hello today i want to talk to you about  the t bioinfo platform a platform  designed for bioinformatics discovery  and it offers scalable and efficient  multi-omics workflows designed for  biologists and clinicians that want to  work with complex and large omics  datasets now the platform is flexible it  can be extended with various scripts and  r and python and it can easily be  extended through packages that we often  use for publication quality  visualization or even with interactive  dashboards so today i'll talk to you a  little bit more about the methods of  analysis integration mathematical  modeling and simulation as well as  visualization that you can use on the  tbi info platform  so the methods are designed to offer  consistent and easy to use workflows for  different types of omics data like  genomics transcriptomics and others and  the platform can be integrated into your  research lab workflow essentially  connecting to your local hosted servers  or deployed onto the cloud for various  aspects of the platform results like  visualization or results in a tabular  format it can also offer  tools for preparing mathematical  modeling and and therefore simulation of  various experimental factors  so the key feature of the platform is  really all about processing various  types of omics data and that includes  taking the data that comes from the lab  and processing genomic epigenomic  transcriptomic proteomic metagenomic  environment and many other types of  omics data and that's why the key focus  of this platform is to enable processing  analysis visualization and ultimately  multi-omics integration with easy  easy-to-use visual  and structural outputs  after extensively testing this platform  with many users we have discovered that  the significant features allow the  platform to be very user-friendly for  biologists and useful for even technical  bioinformaticians that find it extremely  or mostly useful  importantly the outputs are provided in  various formats that could later be used  for downstream analysis in commonly used  tools like python and r  and provide important features for  biologists that would like to just  explore their data or even perform  complex analysis that requires a lot of  resources like ram  or cpu or even gpu  various sections of the platform  could be useful for those that use  standard tools  and large data sets  and also could be adapted to specialized  data data that is developed within the  lab  specific formats of the data as well as  specific types of analyses and so we've  been privileged to work with many  researchers at both academic and  commercial institutions that have worked  with our team as well as the tower  bioinformatics research center to make  sure that these tools can also be  utilized across the whole lab and  reproducible for others to preview and  understand what the analysis results  mean  so as i mentioned the key feature are  tools for reproducible workflow  and that includes color-coded maps for  logical analysis  explanatory pop-ups that show what the  method actually does  it's a guided process that limits the  number of mistakes that one can make as  they build the pipeline and produces  some  interactive or visual outputs that are  easily interpreted for biologists  one commonly used data  is rna-seq or transcriptomic data so we  have a collection of tools to map and  quantify  gene expression  using various tools  like  mapping on the reference genome if it's  annotated generating a transcriptome  and identifying novel transcripts as  well as downstream analysis for  gene set enrichment analysis  differential gene expression and  all kinds of annotation especially for  model genomes  more recently there has been a lot of  interest in single cell rna sequencing  and so we've designed a series of  options for a typical workflow using the  serrat package where you can produce  standard visualization as well as  outputs with an rds file that you can  later on download and and search through  the different objects that were  developed as a part of this workflow  importantly it offers some standard  tools as well as annotation and complex  heat maps that could also be  revised in an interactive way  microbiome sequencing  using hyper variable region amplicon  sequencing data or whole 16s ribosomal  rna data  can be processed using tools like chime  2  data 2 using reference databases like  silva  and many other tools that could be  selected like phyloseq and this produces  static visualizations or a dashboard  that allows us to compare different  types of annotation in the metadata and  identify  taxonomic variation  alpha and beta diversity and explore  variation across  different types of samples or groups of  samples  another type of data that could be  analyzed on the platform is genomic data  where we can process fast q files for  variant calling  and finding differences between  frequencies of mutations as well as  analysis of multiple sequence alignment  with phylodynamics and even mapping of  those results onto protein structures  as data sets grow and become more  complex  researchers utilize more and more data  mining and machine learning methods to  make sense of what is in the data  understand the biological processes that  define variation across samples and  build machine learning predictive models  using different methods and different  sets of features like like biomarker  discovery  or gene set construction and so we've  designed a series of interactive tools  that could be used to  train and deploy these models  also using various features to  understand the source of variation  and the different types of optimal  settings to find those  variations ultimately when all of this  data is generated a lot of times one of  the greatest challenges is to integrate  this data and so because we work with  research institutions like the tauber  bioinformatics research center academic  research in this field  has  resulted in a series of novel approaches  in a series of novel approaches to be  able to map these complex networks of  omix features and find  association in these networks  for critical features  the platform is extendable and so we  have been working with a variety of labs  companies and individual users to make  sure that the outputs are interactive  and could assist the researchers in  finding patterns that are most  interesting to them and so we offer  visualization as a service as well as  custom solutions so that you can make  sense of complex data  we also offer extensive training  using  tools that biologists can use like the  tbi info platform but also  methods of coding and downstream  analysis in r and in python and we have  developed a full  website dedicated to such training  called omics logic so you can explore  the variety of  training courses as well as programs led  by mentors and exemplar projects that  you can  identify ways researchers use these  methods to analyze their own data  over the years  thousands of students from around the  world from us south america europe  middle east  asia  have gone through such training and  we've established relationships with  many universities around the world to  offer such curricula and train students  to utilize tools that they can rely on  to make sense of complex optics data
 

